    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Recap in one minute. RL from a taught warm start works on OOLONG. The route choice is the part RL cannot hold: every remedy we tried, and the literature's, collapses. The pair to stress: SFT can learn the rule; RL from the correct router loses it in two updates."
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "The prelude is new kernel machinery: code bound before the first cell, listed by contract. The untrained 4B calls it correctly on both families. On the 293-row held-out OOLONG set it equals the scripted expert and beats the best RL controller with zero errors and fewer tokens. Teaching the call pattern buys only the cheap lookup route and costs four times the tokens on short tasks."
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Signature from the input text only. Table from teaching and train splits only. The note is a few lines of verified history with one recommendation. 120 of 120 follow it. Best accuracy and lowest tokens of any controller. Cannot collapse because nothing is trained."
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Caution slide. The shipped docstring claimed every form; the controller submitted unknown 72 times. Honest contract: look at the question first, then aggregate. Top-buyer losses are format only. This motivates growth: a verified routine fixes format and budget."
    },
    {
      "idx": 5,
      "label": "6",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Propose, verify on the model's own extractions, register with its record. Two forms recovered from zero to perfect with no training. Third form: all 24 of the 4B's candidates had the same sort-direction bug; verification kept them out; showing it the failures did not help. The 8B proposer, reasoning for about 5k tokens first, got it right on all eight candidates, and the 4B caller is then perfect on dev. Ceiling is the proposer, not the loop."
    },
    {
      "idx": 6,
      "label": "7",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Library and table make caller size irrelevant from 4B to 8B. A 0.6B tuned on 436 of the 4B's own one-cell calls matches them, two seeds; on 293 held-out rows the tuned 0.6B, 1.7B and untrained 4B are right on identical rows. Phi-4-mini shows the recipe is not Qwen-specific. At 360M protocol is learned but the note's routine is misread on two new forms: the first capacity limit."
    },
    {
      "idx": 7,
      "label": "8",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Controller sees metadata only, so a 0.6B handles 2M-token inputs; cost linear. Accuracy falls via count questions, where per-row label ambiguity accumulates. On the paper's own setting (TREC 131K, 50 tasks) we are level with GPT-5 base and below RLM(GPT-5). The official RLM-Qwen3-8B baseline run is in progress."
    },
    {
      "idx": 8,
      "label": "9",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Sharpened claim: capability in the library, selection in statistics, weights call and compose. Limits are real: synthetic, one seed, greedy table. Next tests are small and ready; the harder form and the 8B proposer come first."
    },
    {
      "idx": 9,
      "label": "10",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Only if asked. Splits by seed namespace; scripted replays get a fixed controller overhead in the cost table."
    }
  ]
}
